# Kokoro-FastAPI, the TTS server the corpora are generated from - both the training
# positives/negatives and the evaluation corpus.
#
# NOTHING IS BUILT HERE. This points straight at the published image; the file exists
# so that the two things worth deciding about it live in one place with their reasons,
# instead of being inline strings in docker-compose.yml.
#
# 1. THE TAG IS PINNED, NOT `latest`. A TTS version change changes the rendered audio,
#    the corpus generated from it, and therefore every number measured against that
#    corpus - silently, and long after the fact. `latest` moved four times in the tag
#    list while this pipeline was being tuned. Nothing about the pipeline detects that
#    its negatives were regenerated by a different renderer, so the pin is the only
#    thing standing between a corpus and an unexplainable score change.
#
# 2. CPU IS THE DEFAULT, GPU IS OPT-IN. Corpus generation has to be possible on the
#    machine doing the EVALUATION, which is the Mac - `docker-compose.cuda.yml` is what
#    the training server adds. Both variants publish linux/arm64, so the CPU image
#    runs native on Apple Silicon rather than under emulation.
#
#    The GPU image is a genuine speed-up here, unlike Piper (see the measurement in
#    docker-compose.yml, where CUDA made Piper 2.5x SLOWER). It is a throughput
#    choice, not a correctness one: the two images render the same voices, so a
#    corpus generated on CPU is interchangeable with one generated on GPU.
#
# ARG BEFORE FROM is what makes one file serve both. It is the only position where a
# build arg can parameterise the base image, and it means the CPU/GPU choice is a
# build argument rather than a second Dockerfile that would drift from this one.
ARG KOKORO_IMAGE=ghcr.io/remsky/kokoro-fastapi-cpu:v0.8.1
FROM ${KOKORO_IMAGE}

# Deliberately no server configuration here: the two things this image owns -
# what it fronts (the ARG above) and what it exposes (below) - are the whole
# surface, and the rest is compose's business.
# THE TTS PROTOCOL WRAPPER, IN THE SAME CONTAINER. The trainers and eval no
# longer speak this image's OpenAI-compatible API - they speak the repo's TTS
# protocol over TCP. The wrapper (docker/tts_engines/kokoro_http_engine) is a
# thin adapter that fronts the in-image FastAPI server, so the one container
# serves both: 8899 for the protocol, 8880 for anything that still speaks the
# raw API (the audit tool does).
#
# tts-protocol is installed from the repo's own source first, because the
# wrapper's pyproject deliberately does not depend on it (pip cannot resolve
# uv-style path sources); both installs are --no-deps because numpy/scipy and
# requests are already in the base image, and re-resolving them from PyPI
# here would float exactly the versions the adapters were tested against.
# If the base image ever moves them, this is where the failure would land -
# loudly, at build time.
#
# The copy dance is not ceremony: the base image runs as appuser (uid 1000)
# with a read-only root-owned /opt, and its `python` is a uv-managed venv at
# /app/.venv (the one the entrypoint runs), so these must be installed INTO
# that venv or the wrapper's module is invisible to it - while setuptools'
# egg-info step writes into the source tree it is given, which COPY leaves
# owned by the build host's uid, so the source has to be re-copied into a
# directory appuser owns first. The _src trees are left in /tmp - appuser
# cannot unlink root-owned files and /tmp is ephemeral.
COPY tts-service/tts_protocol/ /tmp/tts_protocol_src/
COPY docker/tts_engines/ /tmp/tts_engines_src/
RUN set -eux; \
    mkdir /tmp/pipsrc; \
    cp -r /tmp/tts_protocol_src /tmp/pipsrc/tts_protocol; \
    cp -r /tmp/tts_engines_src /tmp/pipsrc/tts_engines; \
    /app/.venv/bin/python -m ensurepip --default-pip; \
    /app/.venv/bin/python -m pip install --no-cache-dir --no-deps \
        /tmp/pipsrc/tts_protocol /tmp/pipsrc/tts_engines; \
    rm -rf /tmp/pipsrc

# 8880: the raw OpenAI-compatible API (audit tool, manual checks).
# 8899: the TTS protocol - what the trainers and eval speak.
EXPOSE 8880 8899

# The base image's own entrypoint (uvicorn on 8880) runs in the background;
# the wrapper is the foreground process, so the container's lifecycle follows
# the port the trainers actually use. The wrapper's serve() fails fast on a
# dead backend (the protocol's fail-at-startup guarantee), so the command
# first waits for FastAPI to answer - the same /v1/audio/voices call the
# wrapper itself makes - instead of racing it at container start.
CMD ["sh", "-c", "./entrypoint.sh & \
     for i in $(seq 1 60); do \
       /app/.venv/bin/python -c \"import urllib.request; \
       urllib.request.urlopen('http://127.0.0.1:8880/v1/audio/voices', timeout=2)\" 2>/dev/null && break; \
       sleep 1; done; \
     exec /app/.venv/bin/python -m kokoro_http_engine \
          --url http://127.0.0.1:8880 --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8899"]
